                ```
                <p>
                    Effective conversion optimization requires more than simply changing
                    buttons or page layouts. Teams need to understand customer behavior,
                    business objectives, product usability, and the reasons customers may
                    or may not complete important actions.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Clear Conversion Goals:</b> Define specific customer actions that the optimization program is intended to improve.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Customer Understanding:</b> Use customer research and behavioral insights to understand motivations and barriers.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Simple User Journeys:</b> Reduce unnecessary steps and make important actions easy to understand.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Relevant Messaging:</b> Communicate product value clearly and provide information that supports customer decisions.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Data-Driven Decisions:</b> Use measurable evidence instead of relying only on assumptions or opinions.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Continuous Testing:</b> Regularly validate changes and use the results to guide future optimization activities.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

