                <h3><b>A/B Testing and Conversion Experimentation</b></h3>

                ```
                <p>
                    A/B testing can help product teams compare different versions of a
                    customer experience and evaluate which version performs better against
                    a defined objective. Experiments should be based on a clear hypothesis
                    rather than random design changes.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Teams can test elements such as page structure, messaging, navigation,
                    forms, calls to action, onboarding flows, and other relevant product
                    experiences. The selected test should have a clearly defined success
                    metric.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Results should be interpreted carefully and considered alongside customer
                    feedback and other product data. A single experiment should contribute
                    to broader learning rather than being viewed in isolation.
                </p>
            </div>

